---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { TOWNS } from '../../lib/data';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Aurora Tonight 2026: who builds it and why it answers per town';
const description = 'Aurora Tonight is published by Radif Partners: a free 2026 northern lights forecast that answers yes, maybe or no per town from SWPC, NWS and astronomy, no app.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Aurora Tonight</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Search "northern lights tonight" and the first results give you a planetary Kp index, a map of the whole pole, or a table of probabilities by latitude band, then leave you to work out what it means for your town, often while pushing an app or a paid subscription. This site does better than the first Google result for that search because it answers for one place, the one you type, with a yes, maybe or no and the hours worth going out: it sets the SWPC Kp forecast and OVATION model against the Kp your town's magnetic latitude needs, the hours of full darkness computed for your coordinates, and the hourly cloud forecast of the National Weather Service, and it publishes every rule it applies.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds free tools on public data. It is not a tour operator, a camera seller, a weather service or a government agency, and it has no commercial link with any of them. There is no app to install, no alert subscription and no account. Nothing on the site sells your location or your interest in the aurora to anyone.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What we use, and what we do not</h2>
    <p>We use what anyone can check: the public data services of the NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center for Kp and OVATION, the National Weather Service API and the Norwegian Meteorological Institute for clouds, AACGM-v2 magnetic coordinates for {TOWNS.length} towns, NASA POWER for cloud climate, the Census Bureau Gazetteer for town and ZIP code positions, and astronomical equations tested against JPL ephemeris data. We do not use crowd reports we cannot verify, private "aurora scores" whose formula is secret, or photos presented as tonight's sky. Where a rule is our own choice, such as how much cloud still counts as clear, the method page says so.</p>
    <h2>How the site is checked</h2>
    <p>The engine is covered by automated tests: Sun and Moon positions against DE421, the Kp rules against the SWPC statements they come from, the parsers against real payloads of each feed, the verdict against hand-made nights. Data files are rebuilt by scripts from their official downloads. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> sets out the whole chain and its limits, and the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Corrections, a town you would like added, or a feed that seems wrong: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or the <a href={route('contact', lang)}>contact page</a>. We cannot send alerts or tell you what you saw in a photo, but we read every message about a figure.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
